\chapter{Rate limits and quotas}
\label{ch:ratelimit}

Every provider API enforces rate limits, and some enforce hard quotas:
IEEE Xplore, for instance, allows at most 10 requests per second and 200
requests per day.
The providers already pace and back off \emph{within} a process
(\cref{providers-module}), but every [[scholar]] invocation is a fresh
process---so a daily quota spent by this morning's search is invisible to
this afternoon's, which then fires 200 doomed requests, gets blocked, and
(until \cref{sec:cache-successful-only}) even freezes the empty responses
into the cache.
This chapter adds the missing memory: a small, persistent, cross-session
record of how much of each provider's budget is spent and when it comes
back.

The division of labour follows from what can be reconstructed where:
\begin{description}
\item[Per-second pacing] needs no persistence.
  Two CLI invocations are always more than a fraction of a second apart,
  so an in-process timestamp suffices---though we do persist the last
  request time and consult it opportunistically, since the state file is
  loaded anyway.
\item[Daily quotas and cooldowns] cannot be reconstructed in-process.
  These are exactly what we persist: how many requests today's budget has
  absorbed, and until when a provider told us to go away.
\end{description}


\section{Design overview}

The module has four moving parts:
\begin{description}
\item[Declarations] A provider declares its limits as a class attribute,
  [[LIMITS = Limits(per_second=10.0, daily=200)]], read via [[getattr]]
  like the existing [[MAX_LIMIT]] and [[default_enabled]] attributes.
  Providers that share one API budget (the OpenAlex source-pinned
  providers all hit the OpenAlex API) declare a common [[QUOTA_GROUP]].
\item[Persistent state] One small JSON file per quota group under the
  \emph{data} directory ([[rate_limits/<name>.json]]), written atomically
  and immediately on every change.
\item[The [[RateLimiter]] ] One object per quota group.
  Providers call [[acquire()]] before each HTTP request (it paces, counts,
  and raises [[RateLimited]] when the budget is spent) and
  [[record_response()]] after (it turns 429s and rate-limit headers into
  persistent cooldowns).
\item[The registry] Module-level accessors---[[limiter_for(provider)]],
  [[check_available(name)]], [[all_status()]], [[reset_state()]]---used by
  the providers, by [[Search.execute]] to skip a spent provider with one
  clear warning, and by the [[scholar providers limits]] command.
\end{description}

Instrumentation is per HTTP request, inside the providers, rather than a
wrapper around [[Search.execute]].
The coarse alternative would miss the real quota burners: one IEEE search
pages through results at 200 records per request, and [[scholar enrich]]
calls [[get_paper_by_doi]] once per paper---a 300-paper session is 300
requests, none of them a search.

