\section{Declaring limits}

A provider's limits are data, not behaviour, so they live in a small
frozen dataclass the provider exposes as a class attribute.
We deliberately support only two kinds of limit: a request rate
([[per_second]]) and a daily budget ([[daily]]).
A generic \enquote{$n$ requests per arbitrary window} bucket was
considered and rejected: the two long-window quotas it would serve are
Semantic Scholar's shared anonymous pool (shared with all other anonymous
users, so our local count says nothing about it) and Scopus's weekly
quota (which the API reports authoritatively in its
[[X-RateLimit-Remaining]] header, \cref{sec:ratelimit-record-response}).
Neither can be tracked accurately with a local window, so the extra state
machinery would buy only false confidence.

Note the units: [[per_second]] is a \emph{rate} (requests per second),
not the seconds-between-requests convention of the older per-provider
[[RATE_LIMIT]] attributes in \cref{providers-module}.

<<data classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Limits:
    """Declared rate limits for a provider.

    `per_second` is a request rate (requests per second); `daily` is the
    maximum number of requests per UTC calendar day.  `None` means
    unlimited (or unknown).
    """

    per_second: float | None = None
    daily: int | None = None
@

The answer to \enquote{may I make a request?} needs more than a boolean:
callers want to tell the user \emph{why} a provider is unavailable and
\emph{when} to come back.
The [[quota]] flag separates the two families of unavailability, because
users act on them differently: a spent \emph{quota} means the day's
budget is gone and the provider is off the table until the reset (the
health check reports this as its own status), while a plain cooldown is a
transient \enquote{slow down} that resolves itself in seconds or
minutes.

<<data classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Availability:
    """Whether a provider may be queried now, and if not, why and until when.

    `retry_at` is an epoch timestamp, or `None` if unknown.  `quota` is
    true when the block is a spent (daily or provider-reported) quota
    rather than a transient cooldown.
    """

    ok: bool
    reason: str = ""
    retry_at: float | None = None
    quota: bool = False
@

When [[acquire()]] finds the budget spent it raises rather than returns:
the provider code sits deep inside paging loops, and an exception is the
only way to unwind them without threading a status flag through every
level.
Providers catch [[RateLimited]] and return the papers they have, matching
the project-wide contract that provider errors never propagate to the
CLI.

<<data classes>>=
class RateLimited(Exception):
    """Raised by `acquire()` when a request must not be made now."""

    def __init__(self, provider: str, reason: str,
                 retry_at: float | None = None):
        super().__init__(f"{provider}: {reason}")
        self.provider = provider
        self.reason = reason
        self.retry_at = retry_at
