\section{Time}
\label{sec:ratelimit-time}

All time flows through three module-level seams.
This is what makes the module testable: a test monkeypatches [[_now]] and
[[_sleep]] and can then simulate midnight rollovers, expired cooldowns,
and pacing waits without ever touching the wall clock or actually
sleeping.
The derived helpers ([[_today]], [[next_daily_reset]]) are defined in
terms of [[_now]] so that patching the one seam moves everything
consistently.

<<time helpers>>=
def _now() -> float:
    """Return the current time as an epoch timestamp."""
    return time.time()


def _sleep(seconds: float) -> None:
    """Sleep for `seconds` seconds."""
    time.sleep(seconds)


def _today() -> str:
    """Return the current UTC calendar day as `YYYY-MM-DD`."""
    now = datetime.fromtimestamp(_now(), tz=timezone.utc)
    return now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
@

Daily quotas are bucketed on the UTC calendar day.
This is a guess: IEEE does not document when its counter resets (its
Mashery gateway defaults to midnight UTC, but midnight US~Eastern is
plausible).
We guess UTC midnight rather than a conservative later time because the
design is self-correcting: if the first request after our reset still
hits the quota, the provider's rejection (the [[OVER_RATE]] gateway code,
\cref{sec:ieee-error-handling}) immediately re-marks the day as spent.
A wrong guess therefore costs exactly one request per stale window,
whereas a conservative guess would idle the provider for hours every day.

<<time helpers>>=
def next_daily_reset() -> float:
    """Return the epoch timestamp of the next UTC midnight."""
    now = datetime.fromtimestamp(_now(), tz=timezone.utc)
    tomorrow = now + timedelta(days=1)
    midnight = tomorrow.replace(
        hour=0, minute=0, second=0, microsecond=0
    )
    return midnight.timestamp()
@

