\subsection{Parsing [[Retry-After]] }

A 429 response may carry a [[Retry-After]] header in either of two
formats: a delay in seconds, or an HTTP date.
Both appear in the wild, and so do malformed values, so the parser
returns [[None]] rather than raising on garbage---the caller then falls
back to a default cooldown instead of crashing mid-search.

<<time helpers>>=
def parse_retry_after(value: str | None) -> float | None:
    """Parse a `Retry-After` header value into a delay in seconds.

    Accepts both the delay-seconds and the HTTP-date form.  Returns
    `None` if the value is missing or unparseable.
    """
    if not value or not isinstance(value, str):
        return None
    value = value.strip()
    try:
        return max(0.0, float(value))
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        dt = email.utils.parsedate_to_datetime(value)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    if dt is None:
        return None
    return max(0.0, dt.timestamp() - _now())
@

The value is checked to be a string before anything else: the header
lookup tolerates any mapping, including the mocks in the provider tests,
and a non-string value must read as \enquote{no usable header} rather
than as an exception deep inside a 429 handler.

<<test functions>>=
class TestParseRetryAfterTypes:
    def test_non_string_values_are_ignored(self):
        assert parse_retry_after(None) is None
        assert parse_retry_after(object()) is None
        assert parse_retry_after("30") == 30.0
@

