\section{Loading and saving state}
\label{sec:ratelimit-state}

The state record is deliberately small and self-describing:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[day]] / [[day_count]] ] which UTC day the count belongs to, and
  how many requests that day has absorbed.
\item[ [[daily_limit]] ] the declared daily quota, copied from the
  provider's [[Limits]] on every save.  Storing it makes the file
  self-contained: [[check_available]] and the status table can judge
  exhaustion from the file alone, without access to the provider class.
\item[ [[cooldown_until]] / [[cooldown_reason]] ] a server-imposed
  block (429, or a gateway quota rejection) and its human-readable cause.
\item[ [[remaining]] / [[reset_at]] ] the provider's \emph{own} report of
  its budget, synced from [[X-RateLimit-*]] headers when present.  When
  set, these override our local tally---the server's count is
  authoritative, ours is a reconstruction.
\item[ [[last_request]] ] when we last talked to this provider, giving
  free cross-process request spacing.
\end{description}

<<state functions>>=
def _fresh_state(name: str) -> dict:
    return {
        "version": STATE_VERSION,
        "provider": name,
        "day": _today(),
        "day_count": 0,
        "daily_limit": None,
        "cooldown_until": None,
        "cooldown_reason": "",
        "cooldown_quota": False,
        "remaining": None,
        "reset_at": None,
        "last_request": None,
        "last_updated": None,
    }
<<constants>>=
STATE_VERSION = 1
@

Loading fails \emph{open}: a missing, truncated, or
incomprehensible-version file yields a fresh zeroed state, never an
error.
A broken bookkeeping file must not stop Scholar from searching---the
worst outcome of forgotten state is one round of doomed requests, after
which the provider's own rejection rebuilds the record
(\cref{sec:ratelimit-record-limit-hit}).
Loading also normalises expired facts so no caller ever has to reason
about stale state: a passed cooldown is dropped, a passed header-reported
reset clears the reported budget, and a new UTC day zeroes the day count.
Note what the day roll does \emph{not} clear: a cooldown outlives
midnight, because a server that said \enquote{go away until 01:30} did
not change its mind at 00:00.

<<state functions>>=
def load_state(name: str) -> dict:
    """Load the rate-limit state for a quota group.

    Returns a fresh zeroed state if no file exists or it cannot be read
    (fail-open).  Expired cooldowns and stale day counts are normalised
    away before returning.
    """
    state_file = _state_file(name)
    try:
        with open(state_file) as f:
            state = json.load(f)
        if state.get("version") != STATE_VERSION:
            raise ValueError(f"unknown version {state.get('version')}")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return _fresh_state(name)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        logger.debug(f"Discarding rate-limit state for {name}: {e}")
        return _fresh_state(name)
    <<normalise expired state>>
    return state
@

<<normalise expired state>>=
now = _now()
if state.get("day") != _today():
    state["day"] = _today()
    state["day_count"] = 0
if state.get("cooldown_until") and state["cooldown_until"] <= now:
    state["cooldown_until"] = None
    state["cooldown_reason"] = ""
    state["cooldown_quota"] = False
if state.get("reset_at") and state["reset_at"] <= now:
    state["reset_at"] = None
    state["remaining"] = None
@

Saving is atomic (write a temporary file, then [[os.replace]]) and
happens immediately on every change---both deliberate departures from
the cache layer's simpler choices (\cref{sec:cache-load-save} writes
directly and only flushes at exit).
The asymmetry in failure cost justifies the asymmetry in care: a corrupt
or lost \emph{cache} file costs a cache miss, but a corrupt or lost
\emph{quota} file makes Scholar forget a spent daily budget and hammer an
API that already told it to stop.
A crash mid-run is also precisely when rate-limit events are most likely
to have just happened, so waiting for [[atexit]] would lose exactly the
records that matter most.

We accept one known race: two concurrent [[scholar]] processes doing
read-modify-write on the same file can lose an increment (last writer
wins).
File locking would close it, but [[fcntl]] is POSIX-only and Scholar
ships to Windows via [[platformdirs]].
The undercount is bounded by the number of concurrent processes, and two
authoritative backstops correct it: the provider's own quota rejection
(\cref{sec:ratelimit-record-limit-hit}) and header sync
(\cref{sec:ratelimit-record-response}).

<<state functions>>=
def save_state(name: str, state: dict) -> None:
    """Atomically persist the rate-limit state for a quota group.

    Failures are logged but never raised: bookkeeping must not break a
    running search.
    """
    state["last_updated"] = datetime.fromtimestamp(
        _now(), tz=timezone.utc
    ).isoformat()
    state_file = _state_file(name)
    try:
        fd, temp_path = tempfile.mkstemp(
            dir=state_file.parent, suffix=".tmp"
        )
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(state, f, indent=2)
        os.replace(temp_path, state_file)
    except OSError as e:
        logger.warning(f"Failed to save rate-limit state for {name}: {e}")
@

