\section{Judging availability}

Availability is judged from the state record in a fixed order of
authority.
A server-imposed cooldown comes first: it is an explicit instruction from
the provider and overrides everything.
The provider's own header-reported budget comes second, for the same
reason---when the server says zero remaining, our local count is
irrelevant.
Our reconstructed daily tally comes last, as the fallback for providers
that report nothing.
The declared limit is taken from the caller's [[Limits]] when available
and from the [[daily_limit]] stored in the file otherwise, which is what
lets [[check_available]] work from a bare name.

<<state functions>>=
def _availability(state: dict, limits: Limits) -> Availability:
    now = _now()
    cooldown = state.get("cooldown_until")
    if cooldown and cooldown > now:
        reason = state.get("cooldown_reason") or "in cooldown"
        return Availability(
            False, reason, cooldown,
            quota=state.get("cooldown_quota", False),
        )
    remaining = state.get("remaining")
    if remaining is not None and remaining <= 0:
        retry_at = state.get("reset_at") or next_daily_reset()
        reason = "no requests remaining (provider-reported)"
        return Availability(False, reason, retry_at, quota=True)
    daily = limits.daily or state.get("daily_limit")
    if daily and state["day_count"] >= daily:
        reason = (
            f"daily quota spent"
            f" ({state['day_count']}/{daily} requests)"
        )
        return Availability(
            False, reason, next_daily_reset(), quota=True
        )
    return Availability(True)
@


\section{The rate limiter}

One [[RateLimiter]] per quota group.
The object itself is almost stateless: all quota facts live in the state
file and are re-read on every operation, so concurrent processes see each
other's updates as promptly as the file system allows.
What does live in memory is [[_last_request]] (sub-second pacing needs
better resolution than a file round-trip guarantees) and [[_warned]], a
per-process flag that keeps a blocked 300-paper enrichment run from
printing 300 identical warnings.

<<rate limiter class>>=
class RateLimiter:
    """Rate-limit and quota gate for one provider (or quota group).

    Providers call `acquire()` before and `record_response()` after each
    HTTP request.  Obtain instances via `get_limiter()`/`limiter_for()`
    so providers sharing a quota group share one limiter.
    """

    def __init__(self, name: str, limits: Limits | None = None):
        self.name = name
        self.limits = limits or Limits()
        self._last_request: float | None = None
        self._warned = False

    <<rate limiter methods>>
@

\subsection{Checking availability}

[[check_available]] is a pure query: it reads the file, judges it, and
changes nothing.
[[Search.execute]] uses it to decide whether to enter a provider at all,
and [[check_provider]] uses it to answer health checks without burning a
probe request against a spent quota.

<<rate limiter methods>>=
def check_available(self) -> Availability:
