\subsection{Acquiring permission to make a request}

[[acquire]] does three things, in order: refuse, pace, count.
Refusal raises [[RateLimited]] (after logging one warning per process,
see above) so paging loops unwind without status plumbing.
Pacing sleeps away the remainder of the per-second interval, spacing
against the later of the in-process and the persisted last-request time,
so even two back-to-back CLI invocations respect the rate.
Counting increments the day tally and persists immediately---not at
exit---so a crash or Ctrl-C cannot lose requests that were actually
made.

<<rate limiter methods>>=
def acquire(self, cost: int = 1) -> None:
    """Gate one imminent HTTP request.

    Sleeps as needed to respect the declared request rate; raises
    `RateLimited` if the request must not be made at all.  Counts the
    request against the daily budget and persists the count
    immediately.
    """
    state = load_state(self.name)
    availability = _availability(state, self.limits)
    if not availability.ok:
        if not self._warned:
            logger.warning(
                f"{self.name}: {availability.reason};"
                f" retry {format_retry(availability.retry_at)}."
            )
            self._warned = True
        raise RateLimited(
            self.name, availability.reason, availability.retry_at
        )
    <<pace the request rate>>
    now = _now()
    state["day_count"] += cost
    state["daily_limit"] = self.limits.daily
    state["last_request"] = now
    self._last_request = now
    save_state(self.name, state)
@

<<pace the request rate>>=
if self.limits.per_second:
    interval = 1.0 / self.limits.per_second
    last = max(
        self._last_request or 0.0,
        state.get("last_request") or 0.0,
    )
    wait = last + interval - _now()
    if wait > 0:
        _sleep(wait)
@

