\subsection{Recording responses}
\label{sec:ratelimit-record-response}

After each request the provider hands the response back so limit signals
become persistent state.
Two independent signals are handled:

\begin{description}
\item[Status 429] becomes a cooldown, honouring [[Retry-After]] when the
  server sends a usable one and falling back to a default otherwise.
\item[ [[X-RateLimit-*]] headers] (Scopus and other Elsevier APIs send
  [[X-RateLimit-Remaining]] and [[X-RateLimit-Reset]]) are synced into
  the state, where they override the local tally
  (\cref{sec:ratelimit-state}).  This is how Scopus's \emph{weekly} quota
  is tracked without any weekly bucket of our own: the server does the
  counting, we just remember its last answer.  The reset header may be an
  epoch timestamp or a delay in seconds depending on the API; values too
  small to be a timestamp are treated as delays.
\end{description}

A 403 is deliberately \emph{not} handled here: bare 403s are
authentication failures, and the gateway-specific quota rejections that
hide behind 403 (IEEE's Mashery codes, \cref{sec:ieee-error-handling}) need
provider knowledge to decode, so the provider calls
[[record_limit_hit]] itself for those.

<<rate limiter methods>>=
def record_response(self, response) -> None:
    """Update persistent state from an HTTP response.

    Handles 429 (cooldown, honouring `Retry-After`) and syncs
    `X-RateLimit-Remaining`/`X-RateLimit-Reset` headers when present.
    """
    headers = getattr(response, "headers", None) or {}
    <<sync rate-limit headers>>
    if getattr(response, "status_code", None) == 429:
        self.record_limit_hit(
            retry_after=parse_retry_after(_get_header(
                headers, "Retry-After"
            )),
            reason="rate limited (HTTP 429)",
        )
@

<<sync rate-limit headers>>=
remaining = _get_header(headers, "X-RateLimit-Remaining")
reset = _get_header(headers, "X-RateLimit-Reset")
if remaining is not None or reset is not None:
    state = load_state(self.name)
    try:
        state["remaining"] = int(remaining)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        pass
    try:
        value = float(reset)
        if value < EPOCH_THRESHOLD:
            value = _now() + value
        state["reset_at"] = value
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        pass
    save_state(self.name, state)
@

Real [[requests.Response]] headers are case-insensitive dictionaries, but
tests (and any non-[[requests]] caller) may pass plain dictionaries, so
the lookup tolerates both spellings.

<<state functions>>=
def _get_header(headers, name: str):
    value = headers.get(name)
    if value is None:
        value = headers.get(name.lower())
    return value
<<constants>>=
# Header reset values >= this are epoch timestamps, smaller are delays.
EPOCH_THRESHOLD = 10**8
@

