\section{The limiter registry}

Limiters must be shared: the seven OpenAlex source-pinned providers and
the OpenAlex provider proper all draw on one API budget, so they must
meet in one [[RateLimiter]] (this mirrors the module-level pacing
timestamp they already share in \cref{providers-module}).
The registry keys limiters by quota group and hands the same instance to
every member.
Because the provider protocol is structural, [[limiter_for]] validates
what it reads: any provider-shaped object---a third-party provider, or a
test double whose attribute accesses auto-generate values---may carry a
[[LIMITS]] or [[QUOTA_GROUP]] of the wrong type, and treating those as
absent keeps a malformed declaration from crashing a search.

<<registry functions>>=
_LIMITERS: dict[str, RateLimiter] = {}


def get_limiter(name: str, limits: Limits | None = None) -> RateLimiter:
    """Return the shared limiter for a quota group, creating it if new.

    `limits` only takes effect when the limiter is first created.
    """
    limiter = _LIMITERS.get(name)
    if limiter is None:
        limiter = RateLimiter(name, limits)
        _LIMITERS[name] = limiter
    return limiter


def limiter_for(provider) -> RateLimiter:
    """Return the limiter for a provider object.

    Reads the provider's optional `LIMITS` and `QUOTA_GROUP` class
    attributes; the quota group defaults to the provider's name.
    Attributes of the wrong type are treated as absent.
    """
    group = getattr(provider, "QUOTA_GROUP", None)
    if not isinstance(group, str) or not group:
        group = provider.name
    limits = getattr(provider, "LIMITS", None)
    if not isinstance(limits, Limits):
        limits = None
    return get_limiter(group, limits)
@

[[check_available]] by bare name serves callers that have no provider
object at hand.
It prefers the registered limiter (which knows the declared limits) and
falls back to judging the state file alone---which works because the
file stores [[daily_limit]] (\cref{sec:ratelimit-state}).

<<registry functions>>=
def check_available(name: str) -> Availability:
    """Return availability for a quota group by name, without side
    effects."""
    limiter = _LIMITERS.get(name)
    if limiter is not None:
        return limiter.check_available()
    return _availability(load_state(name), Limits())
@

[[all_status]] drives the status table.
It walks the provider registry (imported lazily---[[providers]] imports
this module at load time, so a top-level import back would be a cycle)
and deduplicates by quota group, then picks up any orphaned state files
so usage recorded under a since-renamed provider still shows up.

<<registry functions>>=
def all_status() -> list[QuotaStatus]:
    """Return a status snapshot per quota group, sorted by name."""
    from scholar.providers import PROVIDERS

    statuses: dict[str, QuotaStatus] = {}
    for provider in PROVIDERS.values():
        limiter = limiter_for(provider)
        if limiter.name not in statuses:
            statuses[limiter.name] = limiter.status()
    for state_file in get_state_dir().glob("*.json"):
        name = state_file.stem
        if name not in statuses:
            statuses[name] = get_limiter(name).status()
    return [statuses[name] for name in sorted(statuses)]
@

Finally, users need an escape hatch: the reset-time guess can be wrong in
the too-conservative direction too (the real quota rolled over but our
file still says spent), and a replaced API key starts a fresh budget.
[[scholar providers limits --reset]] deletes the persistent record; the
per-process flags are cleared too so a long-lived process (the TUI)
starts warning afresh.

<<registry functions>>=
def reset_state(name: str | None = None) -> int:
    """Delete persisted rate-limit state.

    Resets one quota group, or all of them when `name` is `None`.
    Returns the number of state files deleted.
    """
    if name is not None:
        files = [_state_file(name)]
    else:
        files = list(get_state_dir().glob("*.json"))
    count = 0
    for state_file in files:
        try:
            state_file.unlink()
            count += 1
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        except OSError as e:
            logger.warning(f"Failed to delete {state_file.name}: {e}")
    for limiter in _LIMITERS.values():
        if name is None or limiter.name == name:
            limiter._warned = False
            limiter._last_request = None
    return count
@

